From: Leonardo Bornia <leonardo.bornia@toradex.com>
Subject: [PATCH] efi/libstub: do not load initrd= with a forced command line

With CONFIG_CMDLINE_FORCE, the kernel ignores the command line it is
started with. The EFI stub still reads "initrd=" from the load options
and loads that file as the initrd, so whoever starts the kernel image
still chooses the first user space that runs.

On a UEFI Secure Boot system where the kernel image is signed, that is a
way around the verified boot: the firmware starts the signed kernel with
any load options. Ignore "initrd=" too when the command line is forced.
An initrd from a loader, through the LINUX_EFI_INITRD_MEDIA_GUID device
path, is not affected.

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [Torizon OS secure boot configuration]
Signed-off-by: Leonardo Bornia <leonardo.bornia@toradex.com>
---
--- a/drivers/firmware/efi/libstub/efi-stub-helper.c
+++ b/drivers/firmware/efi/libstub/efi-stub-helper.c
@@ -593,6 +593,14 @@
 	if (image == NULL)
 		return EFI_UNSUPPORTED;
 
+	/*
+	 * With a forced command line, the options the kernel is started with
+	 * are not trusted: do not load the initrd they name either. An initrd
+	 * still comes from the LINUX_EFI_INITRD_MEDIA_GUID device path.
+	 */
+	if (IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_CMDLINE_FORCE))
+		return EFI_UNSUPPORTED;
+
 	return handle_cmdline_files(image, L"initrd=", sizeof(L"initrd=") - 2,
 				    soft_limit, hard_limit,
 				    &initrd->base, &initrd->size);
